.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to block-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a block-style YAML document from the DOM subtree that node
names.  A person can read the output.  The function writes a scalar as
plain text when that is safe.  It is safe when the scalar makes a round
trip with no implicit doubt about its type.  In every other case it
writes the scalar double-quoted.  It also writes a scalar
double-quoted when the first character of that scalar is U+FEFF.  At
offset 0 of a document those bytes are a byte order mark and not
content, and the parser removes them.  A scalar goes out plain only
when a YAML 1.1 reader such as PyYAML or libyaml also reads it as the
same string.  A string that holds a tab, a '?', or one of the YAML 1.1
line breaks NEL (U+0085), LS (U+2028) and PS (U+2029), and the string
"=", therefore go out double-quoted, and the writer spells those three
line breaks as their \\N, \\L and \\P escapes, which every reader
takes as the same code point.  A string that holds a C1 control
character (U+0080 to U+009F, other than NEL) or one of the
noncharacters U+FFFE and U+FFFF also goes out double-quoted.  YAML 1.2
does not allow those code points raw, and libyaml and PyYAML refuse a
document that holds one.  The writer spells a C1 control as \\xNN and
the two noncharacters as \\uFFFE and \\uFFFF.  The same rules apply
to a dictionary key.  A list uses the "- value" block
notation.  A dictionary uses the "key: value" block notation.  A nested
structure gets two more spaces of indentation for each level.

The members of a dictionary go out in insertion order: the order of
the parsed text, or the order of the cyaml_dictionary_set(3) and
cyaml_set(3) calls that added each key.  A replacement of the value of
a key keeps its place, and a removed key leaves the order.  A parse and
then a serialize therefore keeps the key order of the source document,
the same in every run.  cyaml_dictionary_first(3) walks the same order.
The element order of a list is always exactly the order in which the
caller pushed the elements or the parser read them.

A float goes out with the shortest of 15, 16 or 17 significant digits
that reads back as the same double, and its mantissa always carries a
\&'.': "1.0", "0.25", "1.0e+20", "1.0e-05".  Without the '.', "1" reads
back as an integer, and "1e+20" reads back as a string under YAML 1.1,
whose float grammar requires the '.'.  ".inf", "-.inf" and ".nan" keep
their spelling.

A dictionary key that is exactly the canonical text of an integer or
a float key, such as "16", "3.1" or ".inf", goes out with no quotes.
It then keeps its numeric type, and a parse of the output canonicalizes
it back to the same key (see cyaml_parse(3)).  A key that a parse would
read as another type or as another text, such as "010", "3.10" or
"true", goes out double-quoted.

A key whose rendered text is longer than 1024 bytes goes out in the
explicit form: "? key" on one line, and ": value" at the same
indentation on the next.  YAML 1.2 limits an implicit key to 1024
characters, and PyYAML and libyaml refuse a longer one.  cyaml_parse(3)
reads the explicit form back as the same entry.

A float always uses '.' as its decimal point, whatever LC_NUMERIC the
process or the calling thread uses.  For the length of the call, the
calling thread runs in the "C" locale through uselocale(3), which
changes no other thread, and the call restores the locale of the
thread before it returns.

The function writes a custom tag of a node (see cyaml_node_tag(3)) as a
leading "!<...>" verbatim tag.  It never writes a core-schema tag
(CYAML_TAG_NULL, _BOOL, _INT, _FLOAT, _STR, _SEQ or _MAP).  A node only
ever carries the core-schema tag of its own type (see
cyaml_node_set_tag(3)), and the quoting and formatting rules around the
value already make sure that the value makes a round trip to that same
type without the tag.

The text of a tag always survives the round trip in full.  The
function writes every byte that the URI characters of YAML 1.2 section
5.6 do not allow literally inside "!<...>" as a percent escape.  Those
are the '%' that starts an escape, a '>', a line break, a space, a
quote, a brace, every control byte, and every byte of a UTF-8 sequence,
among others.  A '#' also goes out as %23: YAML 1.2 allows it
literally, but libyaml and PyYAML refuse a verbatim tag that holds
one.  Every conforming YAML reader therefore accepts the tag,
and a second parse of the output document gives the identical tag
string.  This holds for a tag that came
percent-escaped in the source document.  It also holds for a tag that
cyaml_node_set_tag(3) attached, because that function accepts any
string.

The library allocates the buffer that this function gives with the
allocator that the root node stores.  Free that buffer with
cyaml_serialize_free().  Use cyaml_serialize_free_mp() instead when the
tree used a custom allocator.

node need not come from cyaml_parse*().  A tree that the caller builds
directly with cyaml_create_list_mp(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3) obeys
the same 500-level nesting limit that cyaml_parse*(3) applies at parse
time.  This function refuses a tree that is nested deeper than the
limit, and it returns NULL for such a tree.  It walks the tree with an
explicit worklist, not by recursion.  The call stack that it needs is
therefore a constant that does not grow with the nesting depth.  The
limit bounds the work of one call, not the stack that the caller must
give.

A NULL node serializes in the same way as a CYAML_NULL node ("~\en").
This is a successful, non-NULL return, and not a signal of failure.
cyaml_clone(3) differs here, because it returns NULL for a NULL node.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated YAML string on the heap on success.  The function
returns NULL when it runs out of memory, and also when node is nested
more than 500 levels deep.  The caller cannot tell the two causes
apart, because this function has no separate error-string channel.
.fi
